<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mâcon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mâcon is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a>, prefecture of the Saône-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. A Gallic and Roman town (Matisco), it was the seat of a bishopric and of the counts of the Mâconnais, whose county was sold to Louis IX in 1239. It is the centre of the Mâconnais wine region and the birthplace of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-de-lamartine">Alphonse de Lamartine</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/macon.jpg" alt="Mâcon" width="320" height="135" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luciani71 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_de_M%C3%A2con.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mâcon lies on the western bank of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bresse">Bresse</a> to the east and the Beaujolais hills to the south, about 65 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>. It is the southernmost city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Saône-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>. The town controlled access to the present-day Lamartinien Valley, where the southern end of the Côte de Bourgogne joins the first foothills of the Beaujolais hills and opens the way to the plains of the Loire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlement began as an oppidum and river port established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a>, probably at the start of the 1st century BCE. Known as Matisco, it grew considerably under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, as the large hoard called the Mâcon Treasure shows: it was found in 1764, and its remains are in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. The town was fortified in the 4th century.</p><p>In the Middle Ages Mâcon was the administrative centre of a county belonging to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, at the end of the bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> that led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bresse">Bresse</a>, which belonged to the Duchy of Savoy. Its position made it a possible entrance into the kingdom for Swiss or German mercenaries during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a>. On 3 June 1564 Charles IX stopped there during his Royal Tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and was welcomed by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeanne-dalbret">Jeanne III of Navarre</a> and 1,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a>.</p><p>In 1790 the Revolutionary government made Mâcon the chef-lieu of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Saône-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>. Austrian troops invaded in 1814 and captured the town again after the Hundred Days. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was the northernmost town of the unoccupied zone between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>; nearly eight hundred German soldiers settled there on 11 November 1942, and it was liberated on 4 September 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mâcon has about 35,000 residents. Its sights include the Cathedral of Saint-Vincent, the old Saint-Vincent in the town centre, the Hôtel de Senecé, which houses the Lamartine museum, and a town hall finished in 1751. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-de-lamartine">Alphonse de Lamartine</a> was born in the town on 21 October 1790. The vineyards around it, of about 5,000 hectares, grow mostly Chardonnay, and the best-known appellation of the Mâconnais is Pouilly-Fuissé. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> gives the town a river port.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mâcon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>John the Fearless was murdered on the bridge at Montereau in 1419 by followers of the Dauphin, and Philip the Good allied with England until the Treaty of Arras in 1435, when he gained the counties of <b>Mâcon</b> and Auxerre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Cappeau wrote the poem, &quot;Minuit, chrétiens&quot;, on 3 December 1847 during a stagecoach journey from <b>Mâcon</b> to Dijon.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-holy-night">O Holy Night</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mâconnais, around <b>Mâcon</b> on the Saône, became a Carolingian county in the 9th century, at the junction of the lands of West Francia and the Burgundian kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-macon">County of Mâcon</a></li><li><blockquote>The lords of Beaujeu appear in the 10th century as castellans in the hills west of the Saône, between the counts of <b>Mâcon</b> and Forez and the archbishops of Lyon.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-beaujeu">Lordship of Beaujeu</a></li></ul></section>
